ANN-0575,Q40,What is Byzantine fault tolerance in distributed systems?,CAND-022dec491d7d,papers/Real-time network security: Integrating ANN and dynamic graph-based.pdf,8,Algorithm 1 D-MAGIC: Clustering and Anomaly Detection Pipeline.,"representing the average distance to its 𝑘 nearest neighbors. Nodes with scores signiﬁcantly higher than the population mean are ﬂagged as anomalies. To formalize this, a dynamic threshold 𝜏 is determined based on the statistical distribution of scores:
𝜏= 𝜇𝑠+ 𝛼⋅𝜎𝑠, (18)
where 𝜇𝑠 and 𝜎𝑠 are the mean and standard deviation of the anomaly scores across all nodes, and 𝛼 is a conﬁgurable sensitivity parameter. A node 𝐪 is ﬂagged as anomalous if:
𝑠(𝐪) > 𝜏. (19)
This methodology oﬀers several advantages. First, ANN reduces the computational cost of nearest-neighbor searches in high-dimensional spaces, making it suitable for real-time applications. Moreover, it en- ables the detection of isolated outliers that clustering techniques alone might fail to identify, thereby improving the comprehensiveness of anomaly detection.
In the SAGA method, a node is ﬂagged as an anomaly if it belongs to an anomalous cluster identiﬁed during clustering (Eq. 10-13) or is clas- siﬁed as noise and exhibits a high anomaly score based on ANN analysis (Eq. 19).  Rather than treating dynamic clustering and ANN as separate detection methods, SAGA integrates them into a uniﬁed system: (1) Dy- namic clustering ﬁrst identiﬁes group-based anomalies, where clusters exhibit abnormal behavior; (2) Nodes classiﬁed as noise or weakly con- nected outliers are further analyzed using ANN-based anomaly scoring to detect deviations missed by clustering alone; and (3) A node is ﬂagged",,,,,,
ANN-0576,Q40,What is Byzantine fault tolerance in distributed systems?,CAND-02fe4921f1a1,papers/Real-time network security: Integrating ANN and dynamic graph-based.pdf,6,Table 1 Aggregated ﬂow features and node vector mapping.,"This approach captures the data transfer volume behavior of both clients and servers during communication. For client nodes in 𝑉𝐶, the features are computed as the average aggregated statistics for all client sockets associated with that node. Speciﬁcally, let 𝑠𝑟𝑐_𝑖𝑝 denote the set of all sockets associated with a client node 𝑠𝑟𝑐_𝑖𝑝∈𝑉𝐶. Then, the feature 𝐹𝐶 for this client node is deﬁned as:
𝐹𝐶= 1 |𝑠𝑟𝑐_𝑖𝑝|
∑
𝑠𝑟𝑐_𝑖𝑝∶𝑠𝑟𝑐_𝑝𝑜𝑟𝑡∈𝑠𝑟𝑐_𝑖𝑝
𝐹𝐶∶𝑝𝑜𝑟𝑡, (4)
All nodes in the dynamic graph share a uniform 11-dimensional fea- ture vector to ensure consistent input to the GCN. This vector includes four generic directional features (amount, length, min_packet_length, max_packet_length), six ﬂag_count features, and one time_delta feature. As summarized in Table 1, for Client-type nodes, the generic features are derived from forward (fwd) statistics, while for Server-type nodes, they are extracted from backward (bwd) statistics. This uniform structure ensures feature consistency and stable graph convolutional processing across node types.
By using the tripartite dynamic graph representation (Eq. 1), the model encapsulates the overall communication behavior of each client. The feature extraction process for each node (Eqs. 2, 3, and 4) ensures that the system captures the communication dynamics of network ﬂows in a structured manner. These feature representations serve as input",,,,,,
ANN-0577,Q40,What is Byzantine fault tolerance in distributed systems?,CAND-095c0708b1e5,papers/Kwon2023_vLLM.pdf,2,,"First, the existing systems [31, 60] suffer from internal and external memory fragmentation. To store the KV cache of a request in contiguous space, they pre-allocate a contigu- ous chunk of memory with the request’s maximum length (e.g., 2048 tokens). This can result in severe internal frag- mentation, since the request’s actual length can be much shorter than its maximum length (e.g., Fig. 11). Moreover, even if the actual length is known a priori, the pre-allocation is still inefficient: As the entire chunk is reserved during the request’s lifetime, other shorter requests cannot utilize any part of the chunk that is currently unused. Besides, external memory fragmentation can also be significant, since the pre- allocated size can be different for each request. Indeed, our profiling results in Fig. 2 show that only 20.4% - 38.2% of the KV cache memory is used to store the actual token states in the existing systems.
Second, the existing systems cannot exploit the opportu- nities for memory sharing. LLM services often use advanced
decoding algorithms, such as parallel sampling and beam search, that generate multiple outputs per request. In these scenarios, the request consists of multiple sequences that can partially share their KV cache. However, memory sharing is not possible in the existing systems because the KV cache of the sequences is stored in separate contiguous spaces.",,,,,,
ANN-0578,Q40,What is Byzantine fault tolerance in distributed systems?,CAND-34931d3de4da,papers/Wei2022_ChainOfThought.pdf,8,Tasks. We use the following two toy tasks.,"Results. The results of these in-domain and OOD evaluations are shown in Figure 8 for PaLM, with results for LaMDA shown in Appendix Table 5. With PaLM 540B, chain-of-thought prompting leads to almost 100% solve rates (note that standard prompting already solves coin ﬂip with PaLM 540, though not for LaMDA 137B). Note that these in-domain evaluations are “toy tasks” in the sense that perfect solution structures are already provided by the chains of thought in the few-shot exemplars; all the model has to do is repeat the same steps with the new symbols in the test-time example. And yet, small models still fail—the ability to perform abstract manipulations on unseen symbols for these three tasks only arises at the scale of 100B model parameters.
As for the OOD evaluations, standard prompting fails for both tasks. With chain-of-thought prompting, language models achieve upward scaling curves (though performance is lower than in the in-domain setting). Hence, chain-of-thought prompting facilitates length generalization beyond seen chains of thought for language models of sufﬁcient scale.",,,,,,
