ANN-0588,Q40,What is Byzantine fault tolerance in distributed systems?,CAND-b172d41d7208,papers/Brown2020_GPT3.pdf,7,,"• Zero-Shot (0S) is the same as one-shot except that no demonstrations are allowed, and the model is only given a natural language instruction describing the task. This method provides maximum convenience, potential for robustness, and avoidance of spurious correlations (unless they occur very broadly across the large corpus of pre-training data), but is also the most challenging setting. In some cases it may even be difﬁcult for humans to understand the format of the task without prior examples, so this setting is in some cases “unfairly hard”. For example, if someone is asked to “make a table of world records for the 200m dash”, this request can be ambiguous, as it may not be clear exactly what format the table should have or what should be included (and even with careful clariﬁcation, understanding precisely what is desired can be difﬁcult). Nevertheless, for at least some settings zero-shot is closest to how humans perform tasks – for example, in the translation example in Figure 2.1, a human would likely know what to do from just the text instruction.",,,,,,
ANN-0589,Q40,What is Byzantine fault tolerance in distributed systems?,CAND-ddf647ebf756,papers/Real-time network security: Integrating ANN and dynamic graph-based.pdf,5,Table 1 Aggregated ﬂow features and node vector mapping.,"For each closed ﬂow, we aggregate a set of features that capture essential characteristics of the network traﬃc. These features are sum- marized in Table 1. These include cumulative metrics like the total length and count of packets sent from the source (forward direction) and received from the destination (backward direction). Additionally, we record the minimum and maximum packet lengths in both forward and backward directions. To provide further insight into the ﬂow dy- namics, we monitor the occurrence of TCP ﬂags, including FIN, SYN, RST, PSH, ACK, and URG, to understand the stateful behavior of each connection, in the case of UDP communication, all feature ﬂags are ini- tialized to zero. is important to note that although Table 1 lists ten fea- ture categories, the ﬂags feature constitutes a dictionary representing six distinct TCP ﬂags. Since each ﬂag corresponds to a numeric count of its occurrences, the ﬁnal feature vector for each closed ﬂow com- prises a total of ﬁfteen aggregated attributes: four forward features, four backward features, one temporal feature (time_delta), and six ﬂag counts.
The selection of these features is grounded in well-established in- trusion detection research and empirical analysis of network anomalies. We reference studies, which identify packet size distribution, ﬂow dura- tion, and TCP ﬂag occurrences as critical indicators of malicious activity [39],[40].",,,,,,
