We then compute the mean (𝜇𝑑min) and standard deviation (𝜎𝑑min) of these minimum centroid distances across all clusters. A cluster 𝐶𝑖 is ﬂagged as anomalous based on its distance if it is signiﬁcantly isolated than the average, as deﬁned by:
𝑑min(𝐜𝑖) > 𝜇𝑑𝑚𝑖𝑛+ 𝑇𝑐𝑙𝑢𝑠𝑡𝑒𝑟⋅𝜎𝑑𝑚𝑖𝑛. (14)
A cluster is classiﬁed as anomalous if it satisﬁes any of the criteria speciﬁed in Eq. 9, 12, or 14. serves as a tunable factor controlling the strictness of anomaly detection and is further detailed in Section 3.6. By combining these three criteria, we can systematically detect anomalous network behaviors in real-time. This approach facilitates early identiﬁ- cation of potential security threats or performance issues by leveraging the learned graph embeddings (Eq. 8) and the structured nature of the tripartite dynamic graph (Eq. 1).
The complete process for the D-MAGIC clustering and anomaly de- tection stage is formally outlined in Algorithm 1. This algorithm details the inputs, outputs, and the sequential steps of applying HDBSCAN, cal- culating cluster metrics, computing statistical thresholds, and identify- ing the ﬁnal set of anomalous clusters.
