ANN-0666,Q46,Explain the fluid dynamics of Navier-Stokes equations.,CAND-9e74f5d49c64,papers/Bahdanau2014_Attention.pdf,8,,"A similar approach of aligning an output symbol with an input symbol was proposed recently by Graves (2013) in the context of handwriting synthesis. Handwriting synthesis is a task where the model is asked to generate handwriting of a given sequence of characters. In his work, he used a mixture of Gaussian kernels to compute the weights of the annotations, where the location, width and mixture coefﬁcient of each kernel was predicted from an alignment model. More speciﬁcally, his alignment was restricted to predict the location such that the location increases monotonically.
The main difference from our approach is that, in (Graves, 2013), the modes of the weights of the annotations only move in one direction. In the context of machine translation, this is a severe limi- tation, as (long-distance) reordering is often needed to generate a grammatically correct translation (for instance, English-to-German).
Our approach, on the other hand, requires computing the annotation weight of every word in the source sentence for each word in the translation. This drawback is not severe with the task of translation in which most of input and output sentences are only 15–40 words. However, this may limit the applicability of the proposed scheme to other tasks.
8
Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2015
6.2 NEURAL NETWORKS FOR MACHINE TRANSLATION",,,,,,
ANN-0667,Q46,Explain the fluid dynamics of Navier-Stokes equations.,CAND-acab3dad094d,papers/Real-time network security: Integrating ANN and dynamic graph-based.pdf,7,Table 1 Aggregated ﬂow features and node vector mapping.,"In the ﬁrst method, illustrated in the pipeline using the solid arrows in Fig. 1, we perform anomaly detection based on dynamic clustering of the node embeddings. The steps are as follows:
3.4.1.  Dynamic clustering (step 4) The fourth stage of our anomaly detection pipeline involves apply- ing dynamic clustering to the node embeddings produced by the GCN model (Eq. 5). Speciﬁcally, we utilize Hierarchical Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise (HDBSCAN) [43], a density-based clustering algorithm well-suited for identifying clusters of varying den- sities and detecting outliers in complex datasets. Let 𝐇∈ℝ|𝑉|×𝑑 denote the embedding matrix where each node in the graph is represented by a 𝑑-dimensional vector, as derived from the GCN (Eq. 8). We apply HDB- SCAN to these embeddings to group similar nodes into clusters. HDB- SCAN operates by constructing a hierarchy of clusters based on density estimation, and it dynamically identiﬁes clusters by maximizing the sta- bility of clusters over this hierarchy. Signiﬁcantly, it can also label sparse regions in the embedding space as noise, particularly useful for identi- fying anomalies. After every set of 𝐹 terminated ﬂows, we perform this clustering step, ensuring that our pipeline continuously adapts to the evolving network traﬃc. The resulting clusters capture groups of nodes with similar network behaviors.",,,,,,
